---
const primaries = [
  { main: "Joy", opp: "Sadness", color: "#ffc285" },
  { main: "Anger", opp: "Fear", color: "#ff6a4d" },
  { main: "Trust", opp: "Disgust", color: "#a0c3ec" },
  { main: "Surprise", opp: "Anticipation", color: "#c4b5fd" },
];

const intensity = ["Annoyance", "Anger", "Rage", "Concern", "Fear", "Terror"];
---

<section id="plutchik" class="band band--section">
  <div class="container">
    <header class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Plutchik's Wheel</p>
      <h2 class="display-md">Discrete wheels, run in parallel.</h2>
      <p class="body-lg prose-muted" style="margin:0">
        The VAD space is continuous. Plutchik's wheel is a set of discrete
        categories, and training runs both at once. Annotators tag each prompt
        with a category so intent can route to the right persona policy.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="plutchik-band">
      <div class="plutchik-inner">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Eight Primaries</p>
          <h3 class="display-sm" style="margin-bottom: var(--sp-lg)">Joy vs. Sadness, Anger vs. Fear, Trust vs. Disgust, and Surprise vs. Anticipation.</h3>
          <p class="body-md prose-muted" style="margin-bottom: var(--sp-2xl)">
            Each primary sits across from its opposite on the wheel. Pairs give
            you discrete categories to route on, stacked on top of the continuous
            vector.
          </p>
          <div class="intensity-row" role="img" aria-label="Intensity mapping from Annoyance to Rage to Terror">
            <span class="intensity-chip">Annoyance</span>
            <span class="intensity-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            <span class="intensity-chip is-hot">Anger</span>
            <span class="intensity-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            <span class="intensity-chip is-hot">Rage</span>
            <span class="intensity-arrow" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
            <span class="intensity-chip">Concern</span>
            <span class="intensity-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            <span class="intensity-chip">Fear</span>
            <span class="intensity-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            <span class="intensity-chip is-hot">Terror</span>
          </div>
          <p class="body-sm prose-muted" style="margin: var(--sp-lg) 0 0; color: var(--body-mid)">
            Emotions intensify toward the wheel's core, so the scale doubles as
            escalation detection. It keeps the model from amplifying user distress.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="plutchik-grid">
          {
            primaries.map((p) => (
              <article class="plutchik-card">
                <span class="chip" style={`background: ${p.color}`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <p class="pmain">{p.main}</p>
                <p class="popp">{p.opp}</p>
              </article>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>